      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-2{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}-6?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/9b254387cd5a117e9dbc470b4f71d56399cb8cde9bb5b83b12f956b538383e61.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/e46a5ad8d2f51bc5112c960167879ff352e372bde573834d5394118448a8224c.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units down to give $y=-2{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}-6.$ The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-8)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/4a360d7b90f2c2ea9b561ad53626682ed2b2b2f7459ab8c7bdafe8c02486c9c5.png)",
